I would like to present my A/B classification model for discussing the Collatz conjecture. The model classifies positive integers according to their last digits, while applying each transformation to the entire integer rather than to its last digit alone.
I would appreciate feedback on the reasoning and, especially, whether this classification establishes the convergence claim or whether an additional mathematical argument is needed :  
Explanation of Notation and the A/B Model

- A = {1, 3, 5, 7, 9}: The set of odd digits that can appear as the last digit of a positive integer.
- B = {0, 2, 4, 6, 8}: The set of even digits that can appear as the last digit of a positive integer.
- n: The full positive integer being considered and transformed; this refers to the entire number, not just its last digit.
- r = n mod 10: A notation used to identify the last digit of n. "mod 10" represents the remainder when n is divided by 10.
- A/B Groups: The last digit is used to determine which category a positive integer n belongs to. If the last digit is in set A, n is classified into the group of numbers ending in an odd digit; if it is in set B, n is classified into the group of numbers ending in an even digit.
- Procedure: The last digit is used solely to select the appropriate transformation. The transformation is applied to the entire integer n—the number is not replaced by its last digit alone. Once a new number is obtained, its last digit is identified to classify it for the next step.
- Purpose of the Classification: Sets A and B encompass all possible digits that can occupy the final position of a positive integer. Consequently, this model categorizes positive integers based on their last digit and determines subsequent transformation steps according to the Collatz rules.
